token stream after applying replacements).\n\nThe algorithm steps:\n1. Create list of calls.\n2. Sort candidates by descending score, then ascending start position, then call_id. But note: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Usually if scores equal, then by start, then by call ID. For tie-breaking, ascending start? Probably ascending, as that's typical. But we need to check from description: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" It might mean descending score, then ascending start? It doesn't specify order, but typical greedy algorithms use ascending start for tie-breaking. We'll assume ascending start. For call ID, string comparison ascending? We'll do lexicographic ascending.\n\n3. Greedy selection: iterate through sorted calls, if the interval [start, end) does not overlap with any already selected interval, select it. Overlap defined as intervals that share any index? Since start and end are indices into tokens list. Usually [start, end) half-open. Overlap if max(current.start, selected.start) < min(current.end, selected.end). (non-empty intersection). We'll use that.\n\n4. After selection, order accepted calls by source start (ascending) for reporting. So sort selected calls by start, then maybe call_id for tie? The description says \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So just sort by start. Then collect call_ids in that order into selected_call_ids.\n\n5. Apply replacements from right to left: among selected calls sorted by start descending (i.e., highest start first), for each call, replace tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens. Since we apply from right to left, earlier offsets don't shift because later replacements have higher indices. We need to construct the final tokens list.\n\nEdge cases: If replacement_tokens is empty, we delete the span. Also, we need to ensure that we don't mutate original case.\n\nReturn object with selected_call_ids and tokens.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures: there are three identical payloads? They are same. But we need to compute. Let's simulate:\n\nTokens: t0..t9.\nCalls: a: [1,3), score0.8; b: [2,5), score0.9; c: [7,9), score0.7.\nSort by score desc: b (0.9), a (0.8), c (0.7). Start positions: b start2, a start1, c start7. For ties? No ties.\nGreedy: select b (interval [2,5)). Then check a: [1,3) overlaps with b? max(1,2)=2, min(3,5)=3, 2<3 -> overlap, reject. c: [7,9) no overlap with b? max(7,2)=7, min(9,5)=5, 7<5 false, no overlap, select c. So selected: b and c.\nOrder by start: b start2, c start7. So selected_call_ids = [\"b\",\"c\"].\nApply replacements right to left: first c (start7,end9) replace with [\"<c>\"]: new tokens: t0 t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 t6 <c> t9? Wait end9 exclusive, so replace indices 7,8? tokens[7] and tokens[8] are t7,t8. Replace with [\"<c>\"] so those two tokens become one token \"<c>\". So list becomes: t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9. Then b: replace [2,5) with [\"<b>\"]: indices 2,3,4 (t2,t3,t4) become \"<b>\". Current list length? After first replacement, list length is 9? Actually original 10, after c replacement removed 2 tokens and added 1, so length 9. Index 2,3,4 now correspond to original positions? Since we applied rightmost first, indices before 7 are unaffected. So b replacement still correct: tokens at positions 2,3,4 (which are t2,t3,t4) become \"<b>\". So final tokens: t0,t1,\"<b>\",t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9. Wait check order: before b replacement, list was [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9]. Replace indices 2,3,4 with [\"<b>\"] gives: t0,t1,\"<b>\",t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9. That's length 7. OK.\n\nBut we need to verify that this matches expected? Not given but seems logical.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly. Should import nothing beyond standard lib. Use json for serialization? Not needed, just return dict. The function must return JSON-serializable object. We'll return a dict with keys \"selected_call_ids\" and \"tokens\".\n\nPay attention to exact output contract: array items must be strings. So selected_call_ids list of strings, tokens list of strings.\n\nMake sure to handle empty calls, no selection, overlapping etc.\n\nAlso note: the \"calls\" list may not be present? According to payload? It seems always present. But handle missing? Not needed.\n\nWrite code.\n\nNow produce response: JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing Python source as string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's write Python code. Use a function solve(case). Inside, extract tokens and calls. Then create list of dicts with necessary fields. Sort. Greedy select. Then order by start. Then apply replacements right to left.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    # Create list of call dicts with relevant fields\n    # Might want to convert to list of objects for sorting\n    # Sort: key = (-score, start, call_id)\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    # Track intervals as (start, end)\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        start, end = call[\"start\"], call[\"end\"]\n        overlapping = False\n        for sel_start, sel_end, _ in selected:\n            if max(start, sel_start) < min(end, sel_end):\n                overlapping = True\n                break\n        if not overlapping:\n            selected.append((start, end, call))\n    # Order by start\n    selected.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[2][\"call_id\"]))  # add call_id for tie-breaker\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\"call_id\"] for _, _, call in selected]\n    # Apply replacements right to left\n    # Sort selected by start descending\n    selected_desc = sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x[0])\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    for start, end, call in selected_desc:\n        # replacement_tokens list\n        repl = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        # Replace slice\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nBut note: The replacement uses original indices; after applying one replacement, indices shift? But we apply from rightmost to leftmost, so indices of earlier calls remain correct relative to original list? Actually, if we apply rightmost first, the indices of later (earlier in original order) are unchanged because they are to the left of any changes? Not exactly: if we have intervals that are disjoint, applying rightmost then leftmost works because leftmost interval indices are not affected by any changes to the right. Since intervals are selected without overlap, and we sort by start descending, the leftmost intervals come last. Their start and end indices are in the original token list coordinates. After applying rightmost replacements, the original indices for leftmost intervals remain valid because no changes have been made to indices left of their start? Actually, if the leftmost interval is [1,3) and the next one is [5,7), no changes between 1 and 3 occur from rightmost replacements because rightmost is at 5-7. So it's fine. If intervals are contiguous? Not overlapping but contiguous? Example: [1,3) and [3,5) would not overlap because end exclusive start inclusive. But if they are contiguous, applying rightmost first changes length of tokens at [3,5) which might shift indices? Actually, contiguous intervals do not overlap, but they share boundary. If we replace [3,5) with something, that changes the token list at positions 3 and 4, but then the left interval [1,3) is not affected because its end is 3 exclusive, so it only cares about indices 1,2. The replacement at 3 and beyond does not affect indices 1,2. So it's safe. So algorithm works.\n\nBut we must ensure we make a copy of tokens list before modifying.\n\nOne nuance: In the fixture, the calls have \"score\" as number, \"start\",\"end\" as ints. We'll assume these are always present.\n\nReturn as specified.\n\nNow produce the JSON response. Need to escape the Python source string. Let's write the code as a multi-line string and then escape it. Use json.dumps to generate proper JSON string? But we need to output manually. Let's use Python to generate the JSON? The response is text. We'll just manually escape newlines and quotes.\n\nBetter: write the Python code as a single line with \\n? But the instruction says \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we can have a multi-line string in JSON by using \\n. I'll produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string that contains the Python code with actual newlines escaped as \\n and quotes escaped as \\\". Also backslashes escaped as \\\\.\n\nI'll write the code as a block and then manually escape. Alternatively, I can use a Python script to generate the JSON, but here I must type manually. I'll be careful.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    # Sort calls by descending score, then start, then call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        start = call[\"start\"]\n        end = call[\"end\"]\n        overlap = False\n        for sel_start, sel_end, _ in selected:\n            if max(start, sel_start) < min(end, sel_end):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append((start, end, call))\n    # Order by start\n    selected.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[2][\"call_id\"]))\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\"call_id\"] for _, _, call in selected]\n    # Apply replacements from right to left\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\n    # Sort by start descending\n    selected_desc = sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x[0])\n    for start, end, call in selected_desc:\n        repl = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nNow escape it. Replace backslash with \\\\, double quote with \\\", newline with \\n.\n\nI'll convert to a single escaped string.\n\nBut note: The code uses double quotes for strings inside. In the JSON string, we need to escape those double quotes. Also the backslashes in lambda? None. So fine.\n\nLet me write the escaped version:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        start = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        end = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        overlap = False\\n        for sel_start, sel_end, _ in selected:\\n            if max(start, sel_start) < min(end, sel_end):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append((start, end, call))\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[2][\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\\\"call_id\\\"] for _, _, call in selected]\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    selected_desc = sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x[0])\\n    for start, end, call in selected_desc:\\n        repl = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n        result_tokens[start:end] = repl\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"\n\nI need to ensure indentation is preserved using spaces? In JSON string, newlines and spaces are fine. But the code must be valid Python. The above string includes newline characters. That should work.\n\nNow the final JSON response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"],